# virtio guest drivers, so ONE kernel package boots both on the Odin 3 and under
# QEMU. That turns run-vm.sh into a real smoke test for this package — "does the
# patched kernel actually boot" — instead of only testing userland on a stock
# ALARM kernel.
#
# Most of virtio is already on: the arm64 defconfig gives us VIRTIO, VIRTIO_PCI,
# VIRTIO_BLK, VIRTIO_NET, VIRTIO_CONSOLE, HW_RANDOM_VIRTIO, PCI_HOST_GENERIC and
# SERIAL_AMBA_PL011 as =y. So the kernel would already reach a serial console in
# QEMU. These three are what it lacks — display, input and audio:
CONFIG_DRM_VIRTIO_GPU=y
CONFIG_VIRTIO_INPUT=y
CONFIG_SND_VIRTIO=m

# Cost on the real device is negligible — a few hundred KB that never probes,
# since no virtio bus exists. Weighed against maintaining two kernel variants,
# or shipping a device kernel nobody can boot-test before the hardware arrives,
# this is the cheap side of the trade. Delete this file if you disagree; nothing
# else depends on it.
#
# Note DRM_VIRTIO_GPU is =y rather than =m to match DRM_MSM, which must be built
# in for the bootloader's live panel handoff (see 10-qcom-platform.config). A
# modular DRM driver alongside a built-in one is asking for probe-order trouble.
